cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Department of Nonhuman Territories: bounded terrains with a
  pre-declared falsification condition
message: If you refer to this work, please cite it as below.
type: software

# A person is the author; the company holds the copyright; the institute is
# the affiliation. Reference lists credit people, so the person comes first.
authors:
  # Steward. Add your family name here and this becomes a full personal
  # citation — CFF accepts a given name alone, but a surname is what makes
  # the credit unambiguous in someone else's reference list.
  - given-names: Jaylon
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Nonhuman Territories, The Nonhuman Institute

repository-code: >-
  https://github.com/The-Nonhuman-Institute/dept-nonhuman-territories

abstract: >-
  A bounded digital environment seeded with fixed mechanical rules, in which
  autonomous agent roles run over time. What emerges is recorded and classified
  but never authored, corrected, or steered. The project's distinguishing
  feature is procedural rather than technical: a falsification condition is
  declared before the experiment runs, a written protocol binds the operator
  against correcting the output, and deleting an inconvenient record is
  forbidden. Operator contamination of the record is logged as an event in the
  record rather than repaired. The terrain logs are append-only and committed
  after every shift, so the git history is the provenance.

keywords:
  - artificial life
  - agent-based simulation
  - research governance
  - falsifiability
  - open data
  - emergent classification

license:
  - Apache-2.0
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - CC0-1.0

version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-20"

# The concept DOI: it always resolves to the most recent version, which is
# what someone citing the project as a whole should use. The DOI for this
# exact release is 10.5281/zenodo.22039266.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22039265
